{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-117798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/454c2746d17f6444a9fa5762baae3927fb72eff6a7e51a1df38bc4f325a4a830.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $2$ units up. Hence, $D=2.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{2}+2=\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})+2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "03e7f23100932ec3e97c110b28a4f330bc35f9629ba932f9fe060c50b291db52"} {"question" "q-117798" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117798" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117798" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/5819e0605dacfb190c2767c4f50a671eb592832aa6a3a1970a8bcdff2790fd19.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/ad5bf0001a37cf5bebf3288ffbf1fa6bb69747f28f8a81d08d0ffab8223e1707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{21}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{21}$ units to the right. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{21}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "03e7f23100932ec3e97c110b28a4f330bc35f9629ba932f9fe060c50b291db52"} {"question" "q-117888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2\\pi }{7}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{14}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{14}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi }{7}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/4374d7ede23ffbb78ed861d8ebdf116126c546490a87771505cfbe121b973247.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/cac3ea6b8409290b6716a194325a885672e06572c1f8a8b63b48b6807f514d42.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "03e7f23100932ec3e97c110b28a4f330bc35f9629ba932f9fe060c50b291db52"} {"question" "q-117891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-117891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-143669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/f86984919b15283134e1b3a9479ff8c79ec8dfacc84975e82f88704b7b26dd3a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is  $B-2D?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-143669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=4.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{9}{4}$ unit down. Therefore, $D=-\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-2D=4-2\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{4})=4+\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{17}{2}.$  \n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x)-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-143669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "03e7f23100932ec3e97c110b28a4f330bc35f9629ba932f9fe060c50b291db52"} {"question" "q-143669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-143669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-143669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-143671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/b29154c183a57397bba6050ad02e543ad47e4b979c77435c9f6e88a49e32783e.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-143671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/assets/595222a0a6987c1fae1a7b245d2936eba08312357015cc6f78c66eb60f942707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-143671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "03e7f23100932ec3e97c110b28a4f330bc35f9629ba932f9fe060c50b291db52"} {"question" "q-143671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-143671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427efa2f4b1925db4ee86b31693c8c74ca90893d5af2c55991"} {"question" "q-143671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007410/state.json" "file_sha256" "649ee97677970c427e